      "text": "To completely factor the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the greatest common factor (GCF):**\n   Look at the coefficients of the terms \\(9x^2\\) and \\(3x\\).\n   - The coefficient of \\(9x^2\\) is \\(9\\).\n   - The coefficient of \\(3x\\) is \\(3\\).\n   - The GCF of the coefficients \\(9\\) and \\(3\\) is \\(3\\).\n   - The GCF of the variable parts \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\) is \\(x\\).\n   - Therefore, the GCF of \\(9x^2\\) and \\(3x\\) is \\(3x\\).\n\n2. **Factor out the GCF:**\n   We factor out \\(3x\\) from each term in the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\).\n\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Write the factored expression:**\n   After factoring out \\(3x\\), we are left with:\n\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x + 1)\n   \\]\n\nThus, the completely factored form of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n\\]",
